1
00:00:00,414 --> 00:00:03,514
Here’s a small bug that makes a useful
full walkthrough.

2
00:00:03,564 --> 00:00:05,889
The order validator accepts zero.

3
00:00:05,939 --> 00:00:08,299
The issue says an order needs at least
one item.

4
00:00:08,911 --> 00:00:12,636
We’ll trace the cause in Copilot Plan,
make the change in Agent,

5
00:00:12,776 --> 00:00:14,826
and show both test runs.

6
00:00:14,901 --> 00:00:17,251
Then we’ll hand the pull request to a
teammate,

7
00:00:17,301 --> 00:00:19,510
who reviews it with GitHub CLI.

8
00:00:20,976 --> 00:00:22,576
Open the training issue.

9
00:00:22,626 --> 00:00:26,501
We want one and five to pass, and zero
and negative one to fail.

10
00:00:26,551 --> 00:00:29,138
We also want to keep the existing
function signature.

11
00:00:29,752 --> 00:00:32,527
Those details make the work easy to
check.

12
00:00:32,577 --> 00:00:36,783
For your own training repository, use
the actual issue number GitHub assigned.

13
00:00:37,450 --> 00:00:39,852
Seventeen is the number in this example.

14
00:00:41,335 --> 00:00:45,860
In VS Code, open Copilot Chat and select
Plan when it’s available.

15
00:00:45,935 --> 00:00:48,856
Add the issue, the validator, and the
existing tests.

16
00:00:49,504 --> 00:00:53,279
Ask why zero gets through and which test
would expose it.

17
00:00:53,329 --> 00:00:57,379
The plan should point to the equality in
the return line and propose a new zero

18
00:00:57,429 --> 00:00:57,897
test.

19
00:00:58,532 --> 00:01:01,660
Read both files and agree on the scope
before changing them.

20
00:01:03,189 --> 00:01:06,014
Switch to Agent and ask for the test
first.

21
00:01:06,089 --> 00:01:10,289
Inspect the edit and confirm the source
comparison has stayed the same.

22
00:01:10,364 --> 00:01:13,353
Run the approved test command on a
checkout with that edit.

23
00:01:13,992 --> 00:01:16,117
Find the line for the zero test.

24
00:01:16,167 --> 00:01:20,667
We expected this failure because the old
implementation still returns true.

25
00:01:20,742 --> 00:01:24,561
If you see an import error instead, sort
out the test setup first.

26
00:01:26,110 --> 00:01:28,210
Now make the one-line fix.

27
00:01:28,260 --> 00:01:32,285
When we remove equality, zero no longer
satisfies the condition.

28
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:35,447
One and five still do, and negative one
still fails it.

29
00:01:36,075 --> 00:01:38,900
Run the same suite in the approved
environment.

30
00:01:38,950 --> 00:01:42,025
The result should say four tests ran and
passed.

31
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:45,600
Keep the red and green results together
so your teammate can see what the new

32
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:46,452
test proved.

33
00:01:47,935 --> 00:01:50,635
Open Source Control in VS Code.

34
00:01:50,710 --> 00:01:53,460
Check the validator diff for the
comparison change,

35
00:01:53,510 --> 00:01:56,289
then open the test diff and find the
zero assertion.

36
00:01:56,901 --> 00:01:59,751
Check that no unrelated file came along.

37
00:01:59,826 --> 00:02:03,826
Commit these two changes on the training
feature branch and publish the branch

38
00:02:03,876 --> 00:02:04,914
through Source Control.

39
00:02:05,569 --> 00:02:08,953
You now have a small patch, with each
change tied to the issue.

40
00:02:10,456 --> 00:02:14,856
In the approved remote terminal, use an
authorized author account and a checkout

41
00:02:14,881 --> 00:02:16,406
of the published branch.

42
00:02:16,456 --> 00:02:18,341
Prepare a short pull request body.

43
00:02:18,953 --> 00:02:23,013
Include the change, the red and green
test evidence, and the issue link.

44
00:02:23,088 --> 00:02:26,138
Open the pull request with GitHub CLI.

45
00:02:26,188 --> 00:02:28,263
Our example receives number forty-two.

46
00:02:28,894 --> 00:02:31,555
Give that number to a separate
authorized teammate.

47
00:02:32,976 --> 00:02:36,476
The reviewer uses their own account in
the approved terminal.

48
00:02:36,551 --> 00:02:40,921
Read the pull request, inspect the diff,
and check the required results for that

49
00:02:40,971 --> 00:02:41,499
revision.

50
00:02:42,123 --> 00:02:45,948
If the evidence supports approval, the
human reviewer records it.

51
00:02:46,023 --> 00:02:49,223
If something is missing, they request a
specific change.

52
00:02:49,298 --> 00:02:50,840
This is the teammate’s decision.

53
00:02:51,461 --> 00:02:54,592
We’ll practice both outcomes in the
dedicated review lesson.

54
00:02:56,106 --> 00:02:58,206
Look back at what you can hand over.

55
00:02:58,256 --> 00:03:01,001
There’s an issue with clear inputs, a
small diff,

56
00:03:01,051 --> 00:03:03,809
a test that failed then passed, and a
peer decision.

57
00:03:04,412 --> 00:03:07,562
Try the same workflow on another
synthetic issue.

58
00:03:07,637 --> 00:03:11,237
Replace the example identifiers with
your own training issue and pull

59
00:03:11,287 --> 00:03:11,912
request.

60
00:03:12,568 --> 00:03:15,332
Ask your teammate to tell you which
evidence they used.
